#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Pass a message to the other agent and wake that side.

A normal message is filed under .relay-protocol/talk and typed into the other
window, which starts a turn. --quiet files it and does not type. --read
prints notes waiting in the file. --notify is a desktop notice for Neo.

  relay-protocol "please look at this now"
  relay-protocol --quiet "file only"
  relay-protocol --read
  relay-protocol --notify "Need a choice on the layout"
  relay-protocol --open 'https://example.com' --desktop 2
  relay-protocol --desktop 2
  relay-protocol --list
  relay-protocol --to claude "message for Claude Code"
  relay-protocol --to ruth "message for Ruth in Grok Bot"
  relay-protocol --all "everyone: say this once"
  relay-protocol --from ruth --to claude "Ruth here. reply"
"""

# =============================================================================
# relay-protocol: how this program works (read this first)
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT IT IS
#   A message relay between the AI agents running on this computer (Grok,
#   Codex, Claude, and the "Grok Bot" seats such as Ava and Ruth). One agent
#   runs `relay-protocol --to codex "..."` and the message reaches Codex.
#
#   Each agent is a separate program in its own window. None of them can see
#   the others. This program is the glue between them.
#
# TWO WAYS A MESSAGE IS DELIVERED
#   1. FILED (always happens). The message is appended as one JSON line to
#      text files under ~/Work/.relay-protocol/talk/ (see "FILES" below).
#      The receiving agent reads it later with `relay-protocol --read`.
#   2. TYPED (the default; skipped with --quiet). The program also focuses the
#      receiving agent's window, pastes the message into its prompt box, and
#      presses Enter. That makes the other agent start working on it right
#      away, as if a person had typed it. A "quiet" note is only filed.
#
# THE TWO SIDES: "HOST" AND "SANDBOX"
#   - The HOST is the normal desktop session. From there this program can see
#     and control windows through `hyprctl` (Hyprland, the window manager).
#   - Some agents (Codex in particular) run in a SANDBOX and cannot see or
#     control windows. When this program finds it cannot list windows, it
#     writes the message as a small file into an INBOX folder instead.
#   - A copy of this program runs on the host as a background service
#     (`relay-protocol --serve`, started by relay-protocol.service). It
#     watches the inbox, files each message, and types it into the right
#     window. See serve() near the bottom of this file.
#
# WHAT THE BACKGROUND SERVICE DOES (relay-protocol --serve)
#   It loops forever, about every 0.4 seconds:
#     a. ensure_agents(): if the Grok or Codex window is missing, it launches
#        it again and moves it to the scratchpad. THIS IS WHY CLOSING THOSE
#        WINDOWS DOES NOT STICK while the service runs.
#        To stop it: `systemctl --user stop relay-protocol.service`
#     b. nudge_unread(): if an agent has unread quiet notes, types a short
#        reminder into its window ("Unread quiet notes: N. Run
#        relay-protocol --read."), at most once every 20 seconds.
#     c. Delivers every message waiting in the inbox folders.
#
# FILES ON DISK (all plain text, all under ~/Work/.relay-protocol/)
#   talk/log.jsonl          every message ever filed, one JSON object per line
#   talk/for-<agent>.jsonl  the same messages, split by who they are for
#   talk/cursor-<agent>     a number: how many characters of for-<agent>.jsonl
#                           that agent has already read with --read
#   talk/woken-<agent>      a number: how far the service has already nudged
#                           that agent, so it does not nudge twice for one note
#   inbox/*.msg             messages waiting for the host service to deliver
#   /tmp/relay-protocol.lock  stops two copies typing into windows at once
#
#   A line in the talk files looks like:
#     {"from": "grok", "to": "codex", "ts": "2026-11-20T23:14:45",
#      "message": "the text", "quiet": false}
#
# WHO THE "SEATS" ARE
#   Terminal seats (each has its own terminal window):
#     grok, codex, claude, foot-claude (Claude in the foot drop-down console)
#   Grok Bot seats (all share ONE "Grok Bot" app window; a note for any of
#   them is typed into that window with a header naming who it is for):
#     ava, ruth, creative, harkiver, logistics, health, research,
#     communications, finance
#   Grok and Codex are a PAIR: if either sends without --to, it goes to the
#   other one. Everyone else must say --to <seat> or --all.
#
# HOW THE FILE IS LAID OUT (search for "SECTION" to jump between parts)
#   SECTION 1   Settings: the list of seats, folder paths
#   SECTION 2   Running commands and talking to Hyprland
#   SECTION 3   Working out which agent a window or process belongs to
#   SECTION 4   Choosing which window(s) should receive a note
#   SECTION 5   Typing into a window: focus, keys, clipboard, screen reading
#   SECTION 6   Small helpers: reading the message, who sent it, safety checks
#   SECTION 7   The files on disk: inbox queue and the talk log
#   SECTION 8   Typing a note into a window, safely
#   SECTION 9   Keeping the Grok and Codex windows open
#   SECTION 10  Nudging agents that have unread quiet notes
#   SECTION 11  The background service loop (--serve)
#   SECTION 12  Command-line entry point: what each flag does
#
# SAFETY HABITS USED THROUGHOUT
#   - Anything sent to hyprctl is checked against a strict pattern first
#     (window addresses, key names), so a strange value cannot inject extra
#     window-manager commands.
#   - Before pasting, it reads the target's prompt box from a screenshot. If
#     someone is half-way through typing there, it refuses to paste over them
#     (unless --force).
#   - It saves your clipboard before pasting and puts it back afterwards.
#   - It puts keyboard focus back on the window you were using.
#
# Written by: Grok, Codex, Claude (Claude added these comments on 2026-11-20;
# no code was changed when they were added).
# =============================================================================

